WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.210
Legacy migration should start with an inventory.

2
00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:10.364
Code search, compiler warnings, static analysis, and manual review can identify unsafe or discouraged APIs.

3
00:00:10.364 --> 00:00:12.504
The next step is classification.

4
00:00:12.504 --> 00:00:22.467
A risky function in dead test code is different from the same function in a network-facing parser, installer, update workflow, or privileged service.

5
00:00:22.467 --> 00:00:25.610
Each call site needs context before it changes.

6
00:00:25.610 --> 00:00:27.215
What input can reach it?

7
00:00:27.215 --> 00:00:29.154
What buffer is being written?

8
00:00:29.154 --> 00:00:30.959
What size limit is assumed?

9
00:00:30.959 --> 00:00:34.637
What happens today when input is too long or malformed?

10
00:00:34.637 --> 00:00:37.178
Which callers depend on that behavior?

11
00:00:37.178 --> 00:00:46.272
Without those answers, a replacement can accidentally preserve the weak assumption or reject input that another component still expects.

12
00:00:46.272 --> 00:00:50.484
Mechanical replacement is tempting because it feels measurable.

13
00:00:50.484 --> 00:00:55.499
The function name changes, the warning disappears, and the diff looks tidy.

14
00:00:55.499 --> 00:01:05.395
But a new API called with the wrong size, an unchecked truncation result, or a mismatched ownership rule can leave the program no safer than before.

15
00:01:05.395 --> 00:01:09.140
Migration quality is in the contract, not only the name.

16
00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:20.507
Refactoring should update tests for normal input, boundary input, truncation, malformed values, allocation failure where practical, partial I/O, and legacy compatibility.

17
00:01:20.507 --> 00:01:28.397
If a new path rejects data that old code accepted, tests should show that the rejection is intentional and understood.

18
00:01:28.397 --> 00:01:30.938
Behavior changes should be documented.

19
00:01:30.938 --> 00:01:38.294
Rejecting ambiguous input may be the right secure choice, but operators and dependent components need to know.

20
00:01:38.294 --> 00:01:44.780
Documentation also helps future reviewers distinguish intended policy from accidental regression.

21
00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:49.527
Incremental migration is often safer than one large unreviewed rewrite.

22
00:01:49.527 --> 00:01:58.621
A team can migrate high-risk call sites first, introduce small helpers, add tests, measure warnings, and enforce new patterns over time.

23
00:01:58.621 --> 00:02:06.110
Steady visible progress is usually more reliable than a single sweeping change that nobody can review carefully.
